<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-2-volumes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Computation of Volumes</title>

  <p><alert>Geometry of integrals.</alert> In this section, we will learn how to compute volumes
  using integrals defined by slice analysis. First, we recall from Calculus I how to compute the area
  under a graph as the cumulative effect (the integral) of the height of the graph.
  Given the region under <m>y=f(x)</m> and above an interval <m>[a,b]</m> on the <m>x</m>-axis,
  we slice up the interval into <m>n</m> increments (small parts) of width <m>\Delta x=\frac{b-a}{n}</m>,
  with division points:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
a < a{+}\Delta x <a{+}2\Delta x<\cdots < a{+}n\Delta x=b,
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>and we take sample points <m>x_1,\ldots, x_n</m>, one in each increment.
  This slices the area into increments <m>\Delta A_1,\ldots, \Delta A_n</m>,
  each approximately a rectangle:</p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-5-2-area-placeholder">
    <caption>Placeholder for 5-2-Area.</caption>
    <image width="45%">
      <shortdescription>Boilerplate TikZ placeholder for 5-2-Area.png.</shortdescription>
      <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \begin{scope}[x=1.25cm,y=1.1cm]
    \def\a{0.6}
    \def\b{3.7}
    \def\xi{2.35}
    \def\d{0.45}

    \draw[->] (0,0) -- (4.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.1) node[above] {$y$};

    \fill[blue!12]
      (\a,0)
      -- plot[domain=\a:\b,samples=120,smooth] (\x,{0.55+0.4*\x-0.05*(\x-2.2)*(\x-2.2)})
      -- (\b,0) -- cycle;

    \draw[thick,domain=0.25:4.05,samples=140,smooth]
      plot (\x,{0.55+0.4*\x-0.05*(\x-2.2)*(\x-2.2)});

    \draw[dashed] (\a,0) -- (\a,{0.55+0.4*\a-0.05*(\a-2.2)*(\a-2.2)});
    \draw[dashed] (\b,0) -- (\b,{0.55+0.4*\b-0.05*(\b-2.2)*(\b-2.2)});

    \draw[fill=orange!18] (\xi,0) rectangle ({\xi+\d},{0.55+0.4*\xi-0.05*(\xi-2.2)*(\xi-2.2)});
    \node[below] at (\a,0) {$a$};
    \node[below] at (\b,0) {$b$};
    \node[below] at ({\xi+0.5*\d},0) {$x_i$};
    \node[right] at ({\xi+\d},{0.35*(0.55+0.4*\xi-0.05*(\xi-2.2)*(\xi-2.2))}) {$f(x_i)$};
    \draw[<->] (\xi,-0.22) -- ({\xi+\d},-0.22);
    \node[below] at ({\xi+0.5*\d},-0.22) {$\Delta x$};
    \node[above right] at (2.6,2.2) {$y=f(x)$};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
      ]]></latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>The area of an increment is approximately:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\Delta A_i \ \approx\ \text{(height)$\times$(width)} \ =\ f(x_i)\,\Delta x\,,
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>so the total area is the Riemann sum (see <m>\S4.1</m> Pt 1):</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
A\ \ =\ \sum_{i=1}^n \Delta A_i
\approx
\sum_{i=1}^n f(x_i)\Delta x\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>Taking the limit of very many thin slices gives the exact area as the integral:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
A
\ =\ \lim_{n\to\infty} \sum_{i=1}^n f(x_i)\Delta x\ =\ \int_a^b\! f(x)\,dx\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>But this only gives approximate numerical answers to our question,
  unless we can determine the limit, which is very difficult directly (<m>\S4.1</m> Pt 2).</p>

  <p>No really surprising ideas so far:
  for thousands of years, mathematicians used similar methods to compute areas,
  with very limited success.
  The genius of Newton shines in three ideas which solve the problem,
  easily computing almost any area (<m>\S4.3</m>).
  The first idea is to make area into a function: <m>A(x)=\int_a^x\! f(t)\,dt</m> is the area
  over a variable interval <m>t\in [a,x]</m>.
  Second, since area is the cumulative effect of the height <m>y=f(x)</m>,
  the rate of change of area is the height at the moving endpoint:
  <m>A'(x) = f(x)</m>.
  This is the First Fundamental Theorem of Calculus: <m>A(x)</m> is an antiderivative of <m>f(x)</m>.</p>

  <p>The third and clinching idea is the algebra of differentiation.
  If we can reverse the derivative rules to find another antiderivative,
  a known function <m>F(x)</m> with <m>F'(x)=f(x)</m>,
  then the Uniqueness Theorem (<m>\S3.2</m>) tells us
  that the two antiderivatives are the same,
  except for adding a constant:
  <m>A(x) = \int_a^x\! f(t)\,dt \ =\ F(x)+C</m>.
  Since <m>0=A(a)=F(a)+C</m>, we have <m>C=-F(a)</m>, and <m>A(x)=F(x)-F(a)</m>,
  This is the Second Fundamental Theorem:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
A = A(b) = \int_a^b\! f(x)\,dx \ =\ F(b)-F(a).
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>That is, the area is the integral of a rate of change <m>f(x) = F'(x)</m>, which is
  is equal to the total change in <m>F(x)</m> over <m>x\in[a,b]</m>.<fn>
  The indefinite integral <m>\int f(x)\,dx = F(x)+C</m> is
  the general antiderivative function, also called the primitive function.
  The definite integral <m>\int_a^b\! f(x) = F(b)-F(a)</m> is a number.
  </fn></p>

  <p>These three brilliant ideas, working perfectly together, make it easy to compute the areas of most shapes, provided we are skillful enough at reversing derivative rules to find indefinite integrals.</p>

  <example xml:id="ex-5-2-x-cos-x2-area">
    <statement>
      <p>Find the area of the leftmost region enclosed below the curve <m>y=x \cos(x^2)</m> and above the line <m>y=-x</m>.</p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-5-2-xcosx2-placeholder">
        <caption>Placeholder for 5-2-XCosX2.</caption>
        <image width="30%">
          <shortdescription>Boilerplate TikZ placeholder for 5-2-XCosX2.png.</shortdescription>
          <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \begin{scope}[x=1.55cm,y=1.05cm]
    \def\r{1.7724538509}

    \draw[->] (-0.1,0) -- (2.05,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->] (0,-2.05) -- (0,0.75) node[above] {$y$};

    \fill[blue!14]
      (0,0)
      -- plot[domain=0:\r,samples=160,smooth] (\x,{\x*cos((\x*\x) r)})
      -- plot[domain=\r:0,samples=2] (\x,{-\x})
      -- cycle;

    \draw[thick,domain=0:1.95,samples=180,smooth] plot (\x,{\x*cos((\x*\x) r)});
    \draw[thick,domain=0:1.95,samples=2] plot (\x,{-\x});
    \draw[densely dotted] (\r,-1.95) -- (\r,0.25);

    \node[below] at (\r,0) {$\sqrt{\pi}$};
    \node[left] at (0,0) {$0$};
    \node[right] at (1.05,0.45) {$y=x\cos(x^2)$};
    \node[below right] at (0.95,-1.2) {$y=-x$};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
          ]]></latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>The left corner of the region is at <m>x=0</m>.
      To find the right corner, we set <m>x\cos(x^2)=-x</m> with <m>x&gt;0</m>, so <m>\cos(x^2)=-1</m>,
      and the smallest positive solution is <m>x^2=\pi</m>, <m>x=\sqrt{\pi}\approx 1.77</m>.</p>

      <p>To compute the area, we consider thin slices from the top curve to the bottom line, with height <m>x\cos(x^2)-(-x)=x\cos(x^2)+x</m>, and take a limit of Riemann sums to get:</p>

      <p><md><![CDATA[
A
\ =
\int_0^{\!\sqrt{\pi}}\!\! \left(x\cos(x^2)+x\right)dx
\ =\ \int_0^{\!\sqrt{\pi}}\!\!  x\cos(x^2)\,dx\ +\
\int_0^{\!\sqrt{\pi}}\!\!  x\,dx.
      ]]></md></p>

      <p>We can evaluate the first term using the substitution <m>u=x^2</m>, <m>du=2x\,dx</m>, so
      <m>\int\! x\cos(x^2)\,dx=\frac12\int\cos(x^2)\, 2x\,dx=\frac12\int \cos(u)\,du=\frac12\sin(u)=\frac12 \sin(x^2)</m>.</p>

      <p><md><![CDATA[
A
\ =\
\left[\vphantom{\sum}\tfrac12\sin(x^2) + \tfrac12 x^2\right]_{x=0}^{x=\sqrt{\pi}}
\ = \frac12{\pi}\ \approx\ 1.57\,.
      ]]></md></p>

      <p>This is the same as the area of a semi-circle of radius 1, which looks about right from the picture. Not a result you could get from elementary geometry!</p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <subsection xml:id="ssec-2-solids-of-revolution-disk-method">
    <title>Solids of Revolution: Disk Method</title>
    <p>The integral is a very powerful tool to compute the total of many small parts, such as the thin slices which fill up an area.
    A similar method allows us to compute volumes.
    We start with a solid of revolution
    which is formed by rotating a region in the plane around the <m>x</m>-axis, sweeping out a kind of barrel shape.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-5-2-region-placeholder">
      <caption>Placeholder for 5-2-Region.</caption>
      <image width="42%">
        <shortdescription>Boilerplate TikZ placeholder for 5-2-Region.png.</shortdescription>
        <latex-image><![CDATA[
  \begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{scope}[x=1.35cm,y=1.15cm]
      \def\a{0.8}
      \def\b{3.7}

      \draw[->] (0,0) -- (4.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
      \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,2.6) node[above] {$y$};

      \fill[blue!12]
        (\a,0)
        -- plot[domain=\a:\b,samples=140,smooth] (\x,{1.9-0.27*(\x-2.2)*(\x-2.2)})
        -- (\b,0) -- cycle;

      \draw[thick,domain=0.45:3.95,samples=140,smooth] plot (\x,{1.9-0.27*(\x-2.2)*(\x-2.2)});
      \draw[dashed] (\a,0) -- (\a,{1.9-0.27*(\a-2.2)*(\a-2.2)});
      \draw[dashed] (\b,0) -- (\b,{1.9-0.27*(\b-2.2)*(\b-2.2)});
      \node[below] at (\a,0) {$a$};
      \node[below] at (\b,0) {$b$};
      \node[above right] at (2.55,1.95) {$y=f(x)$};
    \end{scope}
  \end{tikzpicture}
        ]]></latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-5-2-rotated-region-placeholder">
      <caption>Placeholder for 5-2-Rotated-Region.</caption>
      <image width="42%">
        <shortdescription>Boilerplate TikZ placeholder for 5-2-Rotated-Region.png.</shortdescription>
        <latex-image><![CDATA[
  \begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{scope}[x=1.2cm,y=0.9cm]
      \draw[->] (0.2,0) -- (5.3,0) node[right] {$x$-axis};

      \draw[thick] (1.0,0) ellipse (0.35 and 0.12);
      \draw[thick] (4.3,0) ellipse (0.35 and 0.12);

      \draw[thick] plot[smooth] coordinates {(1.0,0.12) (1.8,0.8) (2.7,1.05) (3.6,0.78) (4.3,0.12)};
      \draw[thick] plot[smooth] coordinates {(1.0,-0.12) (1.8,-0.8) (2.7,-1.05) (3.6,-0.78) (4.3,-0.12)};

      \draw[dashed] (1.0,0.12) -- (1.0,-0.12);
      \draw[dashed] (4.3,0.12) -- (4.3,-0.12);
      \draw[dashed] (2.7,1.05) -- (2.7,-1.05);
      \node[below] at (1.0,-0.18) {$a$};
      \node[below] at (4.3,-0.18) {$b$};

      \draw[->,thick] (2.0,0.35) arc[start angle=40,end angle=330,x radius=0.6,y radius=0.25];
      \node[right] at (2.55,0.62) {rotation};
    \end{scope}
  \end{tikzpicture}
        ]]></latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>The total volume is the sum of <m>n</m> disk slices (which is why the method is given its name) at sample points <m>x_1,\ldots,x_n</m> in <m>[a,b]</m>, each with radius <m>f(x_i)</m> and thickness <m>\Delta x=\frac{b-a}{n}</m>.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-5-2-rotated-disk-placeholder">
      <caption>Placeholder for 5-2-Rotated-Disk.</caption>
      <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>Boilerplate TikZ placeholder for 5-2-Rotated-Disk.png.</shortdescription>
        <latex-image><![CDATA[
  \begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{scope}[x=1.2cm,y=0.95cm]
      \def\xi{2.9}
      \def\dx{0.42}
      \def\R{0.82}

      \draw[->] (0.1,0) -- (5.5,0) node[right] {$x$};

      \draw[thick] (1.0,0) ellipse (0.3 and 0.11);
      \draw[thick] (4.8,0) ellipse (0.3 and 0.11);
      \draw[thick] plot[smooth] coordinates {(1.0,0.11) (2.0,0.72) (3.0,0.95) (4.0,0.62) (4.8,0.11)};
      \draw[thick] plot[smooth] coordinates {(1.0,-0.11) (2.0,-0.72) (3.0,-0.95) (4.0,-0.62) (4.8,-0.11)};

      \draw[fill=orange!18,draw=orange!60!black] (\xi,\R) -- ({\xi+\dx},\R) -- ({\xi+\dx},-{\R}) -- (\xi,-\R) -- cycle;
      \draw[draw=orange!60!black] (\xi,0) ellipse (0.2 and \R);
      \draw[draw=orange!60!black] ({\xi+\dx},0) ellipse (0.2 and \R);

      \draw[<->] (\xi,-1.15) -- ({\xi+\dx},-1.15);
      \node[below] at ({\xi+0.5*\dx},-1.15) {$\Delta x$};
      \draw[<->] ({\xi+\dx+0.35},0) -- ({\xi+\dx+0.35},\R);
      \node[right] at ({\xi+\dx+0.35},{0.5*\R}) {$f(x_i)$};
      \node[below] at (\xi,-0.16) {$x_i$};
    \end{scope}
  \end{tikzpicture}
        ]]></latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>The volume of each slice is approximately:</p>

    <p><md><![CDATA[
  \Delta V_i \ \approx\ \text{(circle area)$\times$(thickness)}
  \ =\ \pi f(x_i)^2\, \Delta x\,,
    ]]></md></p>

    <p>and taking the limit as <m>n\to\infty</m> gives the exact volume as an integral:</p>

    <p><md><![CDATA[
  V \ =\ \lim_{n\to\infty} \sum_{i=1}^n \pi f(x_i)^2\,\Delta x
  \ =\ \int_a^b \pi f(x)^2\,dx\,.
    ]]></md></p>

    <p>Once we express our answer as an integral, we no longer consider its geometric motivation: finding an antiderivative and determining the value is a purely algebraic problem.</p>

    <p>This analysis should not be surprising:
    we expect our answer to converge to an integral as soon as we can slice up the problem into small increments.</p>

    <example xml:id="ex-5-2-trumpet-volume">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the volume of the trumpet solid obtained by rotating around the <m>x</m>-axis the region defined by:
        <m>0\leq y\leq \frac1x</m> and <m>1\leq x\leq 2</m>.
        This is the solid of revoltion of the curve <m>y=\frac1x</m> over the interval <m>x\in[a,b]</m>.</p>

        <sidebyside>
          <figure xml:id="fig-5-2-trumpet-placeholder">
            <caption>Placeholder for 5-2-Trumpet.</caption>
            <image width="42%">
              <shortdescription>Boilerplate TikZ placeholder for trumpet side view.</shortdescription>
              <latex-image><![CDATA[
    \begin{tikzpicture}
      \begin{scope}[x=1.45cm,y=1.35cm]
        \draw[->] (0.6,0) -- (2.35,0) node[right] {$x$};
        \draw[->] (0.8,0) -- (0.8,1.25) node[above] {$y$};

        \fill[blue!12]
          (1,0) -- plot[domain=1:2,samples=140,smooth] (\x,{1/\x}) -- (2,0) -- cycle;
        \draw[thick,domain=1:2,samples=140,smooth] plot (\x,{1/\x});
        \draw[dashed] (1,0) -- (1,1);
        \draw[dashed] (2,0) -- (2,0.5);
        \node[below] at (1,0) {$1$};
        \node[below] at (2,0) {$2$};
        \node[right] at (1.45,0.9) {$y=\tfrac1x$};

        \draw[->,thick] (2.18,0.88) arc[start angle=70,end angle=415,x radius=0.14,y radius=0.28];
        \node[right] at (2.25,0.88) {rotate};
      \end{scope}
    \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]></latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>

          <figure xml:id="fig-trumpet-rotated">
            <caption></caption>
            <image>
              <shortdescription>fig rotated trumpet</shortdescription>
              <latex-image><![CDATA[
                \begin{tikzpicture}
      \begin{scope}[x=1.45cm,y=1.35cm]
        \draw[->] (0.6,0) -- (2.35,0) node[right] {$x$};
        \draw[->] (0.8,-1.25) -- (0.8,1.25) node[above] {$y$};

        \draw[thick,domain=1:2,samples=140,smooth] plot (\x,{1/\x});
        \draw[thick,domain=1:2,samples=140,smooth] plot (\x,{-1/\x});
        \node[right] at (1.45,0.9) {$y=\tfrac1x$};

        \draw[->,thick] (2.18,0.88) arc[start angle=70,end angle=415,x radius=0.14,y radius=0.28];
        \node[right] at (2.25,0.88) {rotate};
      \end{scope}
    \end{tikzpicture}]]>
              </latex-image>
              
            </image>
          </figure>

        </sidebyside>

        <p>Applying our formula:</p>

        <p><md><![CDATA[
  V
  \ =
  \int_1^2\pi\left(\tfrac1x\right)^2\,dx
  \ =
  \int_1^2\pi x^{-2}\,dx
        ]]></md></p>

        <p><md><![CDATA[
  \ =
  \left[-\pi x^{-1}\right]_{x=1}^{x=2}
  \ =
  (-\pi 2^{-1})-(-\pi 1^{-1})
  \ = \ \tfrac{\pi}{2}\,.
        ]]></md></p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-5-2-y-axis-rotation">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the volume of the solid obtained by rotating
        the region defined by
        <m>x^2\leq y\leq 2</m> and <m>x\geq 1</m>,
        around the vertical axis <m>x=1</m>.</p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-5-2-y-rotation-placeholder">
          <caption>Placeholder for 5-2-Y-Rotation.</caption>
          <image width="60%">
            <shortdescription>Boilerplate TikZ placeholder for 5-2-Y-Rotation.png.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image><![CDATA[
  \begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{scope}[x=2.2cm,y=1.65cm]
      \def\ys{1.55}

      \draw[->] (0.85,0.6) -- (1.52,0.6) node[right] {$x$};
      \draw[->] (0.95,0.8) -- (0.95,2.22) node[above] {$y$};

      \fill[blue!12]
        (1,1)
        -- (1,2)
        -- ({sqrt(2)},2)
        -- plot[domain=sqrt(2):1,samples=120,smooth] (\x,{\x*\x})
        -- cycle;

      \draw[thick] (1,0.9) -- (1,2.1);
      \draw[thick] (1,2) -- ({sqrt(2)},2);
      \draw[thick,domain=1:1.48,samples=140,smooth] plot (\x,{\x*\x});

      \draw[densely dotted] (1,1) -- ({sqrt(2)},1);
      \draw[orange!70!black,thick] (1,\ys) -- ({sqrt(\ys)},\ys);

      \node[below] at (1,0.6) {$1$};
      \node[left] at (0.95,1) {$1$};
      \node[left] at (0.95,2) {$2$};
      \node[above right] at (1.28,1.73) {$y=x^2$};
      \node[right] at (1.34,2.03) {$y=2$};
      \node[above] at (1.16,\ys) {$\sqrt{y_i}-1$};
      \node[left] at (1,1.74) {$x=1$};
      \draw[->,thick] (1.03,1.25) arc[start angle=-20,end angle=250,x radius=0.07,y radius=0.24];
    \end{scope}
  \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]></latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>The setup is different from the <m>x</m>-axis rotation, so
        we must repeat our slice analysis.
        Since we rotate around the vertical line <m>x=1</m>,
        we should take horizontal slices positioned by their height <m>y</m>,
        and we should write our region as lying next to the interval
        <m>y\in [1,2]</m> between the curves <m>x=1</m> and <m>x=\sqrt y</m>.</p>

        <p>Our slices are thin horizontal disks at sample heights
        <m>y_1,\ldots, y_n</m> in the interval <m>y\in[1,2]</m>.
        The thickness of each disk is <m>\Delta y</m>.
        The radius at height <m>y_i</m> is the horizontal distance
        from the axis <m>x=1</m> to the curve <m>x=\sqrt y</m>;
        this distance is <m>\sqrt{y_i}-1</m>. The volume of each disk is
        <m>\Delta V_i\approx\pi (\sqrt{y_i}{-}1)^2\,\Delta y</m>, and:</p>

        <p><md><![CDATA[
  V
  \ =
  \int_1^2\!\!\pi\!\left(\sqrt{y}{-}1\right)^2\,dy
  \ =
  \int_1^2\!\!\pi(y{-}2\sqrt{y}{+}1)\,dy
  \ =
  \left[\pi\!\left(\tfrac12 y^2{-}\tfrac43y^{3/2}{+}y\right)\right]_{y=1}^{y=2}
  \ =\ \pi\! \left(\tfrac{23}{6}{-}\tfrac{8\sqrt2}3\right)\!.
        ]]></md></p>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="ssec-solids-of-revolution-washer-method">
    <title>Solids of Revolution: Washer Method</title>
    <p>
      One difficulty with computing the volume of a solid of revolution that is obtained by rotating a curve <m>y=f(x)</m> about the <m>y</m>-axis
      is that one typically needs to compute the inverse function <m>x=f^{-1}(y)</m> to express the radius of the disk as a function of <m>y</m>.
      In the washer method, instead of drawing small rectangular slices perpendicular to the axis of rotation, we draw small rectangular slices parallel to the axis of rotation.
      Rotating a slice parallel to the axis of rotation produces a cylindrical shell with a hole in the middle, resembling a washer. To calculate the volume of this washer, we 
      can imagine unravelling it into the shape of a box.
    </p>
    <sidebyside>
      <figure xml:id="fig-washer">
        <caption>Washer formed by rotating a slice of the <m>y=f(x)</m> about the <m>y</m>-axis</caption>
        <image>
          <shortdescription>Illustration of a washer obtained by revolving a rectangle parallel to the y-axis about the y-axis</shortdescription>
          
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-washer-unravelled">
        <caption>Unravelled washer</caption>
        <image>
          <shortdescription>Illustration of the washer from previous figure unravelled into an almost rectangular box</shortdescription>
          
        </image>
      </figure>
    </sidebyside>
    
    <p>
      While one side will be longer than the other, we will study
      what happens as the thickness approaches <m>0</m>, and thus the difference between the actual volume and the approximated volume becomes negligible. 
      In this light, the height of the washer is given by the height of the function, which is <m>f(x)</m>. The width is of the box is given by the thickness of the slice, which is <m>\Delta x</m>. 
      For the length, recall that it came from unravelling a circle of radius <m>x</m> to a straight line, and thus is given by the circumference of the circle, which is <m>2\pi x</m>. 
      Thus, the volume of a thin washer is approximately 
      <md>
        <mrow>\text{length} \times \text{width} \times \text{height} \amp = 2\pi x \times \Delta x \times f(x) </mrow>
        <mrow>\amp = 2\pi x f(x) \Delta x </mrow>
      </md>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The volume of such an object can be computed as <m>\text{Area of Annulus} \times \text{height}</m>.
      For an annulus with inner radius <m>r</m> and outer radius <m>R</m>, the area is <m>\pi R^2-\pi r^2 = \pi(R^2 - r^2)</m>.

      In our situation, the height is <m>f(x)</m>, the outer radius is <m>x + \Delta x</m>, and inner radius is <m>x</m>. Thus, the volume of a thin washer is
      <md>
        <mrow>V_{thin washer} \amp = \pi((x + \Delta x)^2 - x^2)f(x) = \pi((x^2 + 2x\Delta x + (\Delta x)^2) - x^2)f(x) = \pi(2x\Delta x + (\Delta x)^2)f(x)</mrow>
        <mrow> \amp 2\pi x f(x) \Delta x + \pi f(x) (\Delta x)^2 \approx 2\pi x f(x) \Delta x,</mrow>
      </md>
      where we have neglected the <m>(\Delta x)^2</m> term since it is negligible compared to the other term. 
      
      Doing the same Riemann sum setup as before, we get that

      <md>
        V_{solid} \approx \sum V_{slice} = \sum\limits_{i} 2\pi x_i f(x_i) \Delta x_i,
      </md>
      <p>and thus taking a limit as <m>\Delta x_i \to 0</m> gives</p>
      <md>
        V_{solid} = \int_a^b 2\pi x f(x) dx.
      </md>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="ssec-solids-with-specified-cross-sections">
    <title>Solids with Specified Cross Sections</title>
    <p>Consider a solid rather like the sail
    of the man-o-war jellyfish. It has a horizontal base outlined by the circle
    <m>x^2+y^2= 1</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane, and its vertical cross section above each line <m>x=c</m>
    is an isosceles right triangle, with height equal to half its base length.</p>

    <image source="assets/ch-2/portuguese-man-o-war-facts.jpg" width="50%"></image>

    <p>The slices are defined over <m>x\in[-1,1]</m>, and each slice at <m>x=x_i</m> has a base segment
    reaching from <m>y=-\sqrt{1-x^2}</m> to <m>y=\sqrt{1-x^2}</m>, a thickness <m>\Delta x</m>,
    and an area <m>\frac12</m>(base)<m>\times</m>(height) <m>= \tfrac12!\left(!2\sqrt{1{-}x_i^2}\,\right)!\!\!\left(!\sqrt{1{-}x_i^2}\,\right)</m>.
    Thus:</p>

    <p><md><![CDATA[
  V
  \ =
  \int_{-1}^1\!\tfrac12\!\left(\!2\sqrt{1{-}x^2}\right)\!\!\left(\!\sqrt{1{-}x^2}\right)\,dx
  \ =
  \int_{-1}^1\!\! 1{-}x^2\,dx
  \ =
  \left[\vphantom{\sum}x {-} \tfrac13x^3\right]_{x=-1}^{x=1}
  =\ \tfrac43.
    ]]></md></p>

    <p><alert>Method of slice analysis.</alert>
    The goal is to compute the size or bulk <m>S</m> of a geometric object:
    its length, area, volume, mass, energy, etc.</p>

    <p><ol>
      <li>Cut the object into thin slices whose position is determined by
      some variable <m>x\in[a,b]</m>, often slices perpendicular to the <m>x</m>-axis.</li>
      <li>Compute the approximate size of the slice with thickness <m>\Delta x</m>
      near a particular value of <m>x</m> as: <m>\Delta S \approx f(x)\,\Delta x</m>.
      Summing up slices gives the total size, and the limit of thinner slices gives the integral:
      <md><![CDATA[
  S  =\ \int_a^b\!\! f(x)\,dx\,.
      ]]></md>
      The size is the accumulation of the slice measurement <m>f(x)</m>:
      e.g. area is accumulation of slice height, volume is accumulation of slice area.</li>
      <li>Evaluate the integral <m>S=\int_a^b\!f(x)\,dx</m> algebraically or numerically.</li>
    </ol></p>
  </subsection>
</section>